  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The metal halide can be manganese dichloride. It gives white precipitate of manganese hydroxide.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>MnCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>.</mo><mi>NaOH</mi></mrow></mover><munder><mrow><mi>Mn</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfenced></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><mi>NaCl</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></mfenced></munder></math></p><p>The white precipitate manganese hydroxide on reaction with lead dioxide and concentrated sulphuric acid gives purple coloured solution permanganate.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mn</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><munderover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow><mi>Conc</mi><mo>.</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>heat</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>PbO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mi>excess</mi></mfenced></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>MnO</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mfenced><mrow><mi>purple</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>solution</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>The sodium chloride gives&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;on reaction with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>MnO</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>conc</mi><mo>.</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>. The chlorine formed can oxidise iodide to iodine which gives blue colouration with starch paper.</p></blockquote>",
